Yes - e.g., drivers and installable file systems.

 Why isn't there any alternative and to what?
>
> Very good point: the language doesn't help anyone from making
> certain mistakes or finding certain improvments. But a good
> language supports you in accomplishing what you want to do in
> shorter time with better quality.

Quality is not, as I said, something I have difficulty achieving.  And I
find having to think, in detail, about what I'm doing contributes to the
quality of the result in ways other than just having correct code.

 This is not a point in favour
> of C/C++.
>
> Talking about competence: There isn't any problem which cannot
> be solved better in a language different from C and C++.

How about the problem that the rest of the kernel is written in that
language and maintained by people primarily familiar with that language?
Until you can change that, that's a major C/C++ advantage in *practice*,
regardless of whether you believe that *if* that kernel had been written in
another language it would be better.

Unlike academics, many of us have to deal with things as they are, not as we
might wish they were.  It gives one a somewhat different slant on the art:
one is grateful for things that work, even if they're less than elegant, and
appreciates tools that, while they may be dangerous, are also capable of
doing whatever job needs to be done if placed in competent hands.

A language is just a tool.  If it can be used effectively, it doesn't
deserve to be called crap, even if some other language might be safer and/or
more elegant and/or easier to use.  When better languages have replaced
C/C++ to the extent that C/C++ have replaced assembler, then it *would* be
reasonable to call C/C++ 'verging on obsolete' - or at least too esoteric
for common use.  Until that time, C/C++ contribute significantly to the
industry, and form one cornerstone of the open-source effort you appear to
appreciate.

- bill





