Ok, you know that the KZTSA is a differential device, right?  This is the
old, high-voltage differential SCSI that is NOT the same as current LVD,
and not compatible with anything else.  You either need a drive with a HVD
interface, or you need a diff to single-ended converter.  A passive cable
adapter won't do.  Does your cyberguys.com thing do the job?

The main reasons to use the KZTSA are to support multi-host SCSI on VMS,
to support a very long SCSI bus, or if you need to push a lot of IOs
through and old system.  This is the highest-performance SCSI adapter in
Turbochannel, but it isn't terribly plug-and-play -- HVD drives are rare.

Typical diff to SE converters are the DWZZA (diff to narrow), DWZZB (diff
to wide), and DWZZC (diff to ultra).  The DWZZC at least is still a
current product, and is supposed to be back-compatible, but I've never
seen one.

