> I haven't been able to play with it any more.

Could you point me to the docs you've found on the web?

Turbochannel devices (almost?) always have built-in help.  Here's some
console output from a KZTSA in slot 1:

>>> t tc1 ?
       post
       eerom
       sram
       i960
       ticc
       scsi
       reset
       devself
       logs
       setid
       devparams

These are some of the commands it knows.

>>> t tc1 ls
     28 1 boot --> code
     28 1 cnfg --> code
     28 1 init --> code
     28 1 t --> code
     36 1 pst-q
     52 1 pst-t
     72 1 pst-m
  15392 1 code*

This is a listing of the module's ROM objects.  boot, cnfg, init, and t
are code routines.  pst-q, pst-t, pst-m are little files you can list. Any
of these are executable via t tc1 whatever.

>>> t tc1 cat pst-q
   will show you the contents of that file, for example.  I don't have the
captured output handy.


I also have the KZTSA installation and user guide as a 270 kB PDF file.  I
don't know a web location for it; I got it via email.  I can send you a
copy as an attachment if you want it -- send me an email.  It has some
module info, and several bus configuration examples.

Briefly:
  The 5 SIP sockets are for termination resistors for the differential
bus.  If your KZTSA is mid-bus, or you have an external terminator, you
need to remove these resistors.

   Jumpers:
W1 is an in-line fuse.
W2, W4 are for manufacturing use only.
W3 enables terminator power for the scsi bus.  If nothing else provides
term power, you will need to install W3.  It won't hurt if you supply term
power at more than one point, and on a long bus it might help.

   You need to terminate your diff SCSI bus at each end, and nowhere
else.  The KZTSA can terminate, if it's at the end.  If you use two
KZTSAs, one at each end, that's all the termination you need.  With two
(or more) make sure they all have different SCSI IDs.  Use the "T TC#
SETID <id>" command if you need to change it, and then do an INIT to make
it take effect.

If you have diff-SE converters, the SE bus is separate, and needs it's own
termination at each end.

Is your KZTSA firmware reasonably up to date?  I think version A11 is
current, but the documented minimum for VMS multihost operation is A10-1. 
Don't know what Unix expects.  The same firmware kits that update the DEC
3000 system firmware can also do the KZTSA and several other options.

What OS are you using?

