Well, the C if is particularly weak, and brackets for statement grouping
are error-prone.  The Ada if is more robust and expressive.  In Ada, small
syntax errors are easier for the compiler to isolate and flag.  The
"elsif" construct reduces nesting depth in many situations.

The if statement was one of the many points where the Ada designers tried
to correct syntactic blunders from previous languages.

"elsif" is an interesting example.  "else if" and "elsif" mean quite
different things (the former starts another level of nesting).  They could
have chosen "elseif" instead of "elsif", since blanks are significant in
Ada.  But that would have been hell on all the Fortran programmers, who
can't tell the difference between ELSEIF and ELSE IF.  In Ada, where line
breaks are NOT significant, omitting the space might have been correct, or
and error, but the compiler would not know which until the end of the
whole block-if construct.  The strangely-spelled "elsif" localizes what is
a common typo for folks used to some other languages.


> Even still, I would probably use other constructs like
> a switch case statement or something.

I'd tend to agree with you in C, where the if is feeble.  In Ada, the
if-elsif-else construct is the right one in most cases.  In Larry's
example, where the IF involves 3 different variables (WINE, SIZE, and
AGE), CASE won't work.

And if you need a nested if-else in both the if part and the else part, a
switch isn't simple anymore.

