bill@triangle.cs.uofs.edu (Bill Gunshannon) writes:

>  Most languages that expect to see
> use as more than application languages that did not have these features
> end out having a vendor put them in as non-standard extensions (such 
> as Pascal).  Ada may have standardized them, but it still put them
> in.  It just didn't make them visible or intuitively obvious. 

The important difference is it makes you explicitly say so, instead of strange
things (like a missing break or an if =) happening by default or accident. Ada
doesn't stop a broken development process and management from wasting over a
billion Euro on Ariane 501, but the programmers documented, in their code,
what they had been thinking of when they wrote it, and a simple code review
would have uncovered that a number of the assumptions behind them were no
longer valid.

A similar example: VMS's security defaults are "safe out of the box" - you
have to work to open up and make a VMS system unsafe. By contrast, WNT is
unsafe out of the box, and in fact so many pieces of software (including the
OS) assume this remains so that it is a very difficult and reinstallation-
prone activity to try to secure it; even then, some structural unsafeness
remains (DLL hell, for instance). 

This is just bad engineering. In the case of C, a lot is the result of
ignoring, if you want a grand word, cognitive psychology or, more down-
to-earth, just common sense. See the Ada "elseif" vs "elsif" example 
mentioned elsewhere.

	Jan


