In article <rdeininger-2802010128330001@user-2iveaji.dialup.mindspring.com>, rdeininger@mindspring.com (Robert Deininger) writes:

> And if you need a nested if-else in both the if part and the else part, a
> switch isn't simple anymore.

But something like:

	if WINE = RED
	then
		if MOON = FULL
		then
		    ACTION_1;
		else
		    ACTION_2;
		end if;
	elsif SIZE > 500
	then
		if SEASON = SPRING
		then
		    ACTION_3;
		else
		    ACTION_4;
		end if;
	else
		if OS = VMS
		then
		    ACTION_5;
		else
		    ACTION_6;
		end if;
	end if;

Can be deconstructed into:

	if WINE = RED and MOON = FULL
	then
	    ACTION_1;
	elsif WINE = RED
	then
	    ACTION_2;
	elsif SIZE > 500 and SEASON = SPRING
	then
	    ACTION_3;
	elsif SIZE > 500
	then
	    ACTION_4;
	elsif OS = VMS
	    ACTION_5;
	else
	    ACTION_6;
	end if;

Remember that at this level it is the job of the compiler
and not the programmer to perform optimization.  The two
examples above are quite likely to produce the same machine
code (presuming I made no error in deconstruction).


