Robert Dewar seems to take particular delight in showing non-kneejerk
reactions.  As a compiler vendor he hears lots of requirements, above
and beyond his personal experience.  Indeed state machines are one of
his most frequent examples for "never say 'never'".

> I guess your concept of a "small system" differs from mine.  The smallest
> system I have ever seen a validated Ada compiler (and remember, there are
> no subsets of Ada!!) on was a VAX.  At the time I saw my first Ada compiler
> I was doing a lot of work on PDP-11's, Z80 and M68000 microcomputers and
> even a Univac 1100.  I know there was never an Ada compiler for the first
> three and I never saw one for the Univac.

But those using Ada to _target_ small systems are cross-compiling from
something larger (like a VAX :-).  But many are also doing so from a
Windows machine.

> |> for example.  Of course the compiler implementor has to deal with the
> |> whole language.  Ada is also more specific than C -- the implementor has
> |> much less discretion in how a feature must behave.
> 
> Not true, and this is one of my main complaints.  Look up tasking.  It
> is totally implementor dependant.  Two "parallel" tasks need not run in
> parallel and may in fact run asynchronously under some implementations.
> Which enters into a whole other argument about wether or not tasking
> belongs in a language at all, as opposed to being a function of the OS.

Tasks must conform to synchronization rules of the language, and as you
say they are free from there on out.  But tasking is _critical_ for code
that is to be portable between operating systems.

> |> I don't think this is quite the right description of Ada.  It is designed
> |> to let the compiler catch a lot of mistakes, so they never get into the
> |> running program.  This may have a side effect of making programmers a bit
> |> more careful, thus reducing mistakes.
> 
> Experience has shown that it has quite the opposite effect. Why desk check
> your code when you can rely on the compiler to do it for you.  The result
> is sloppier code that is syntactically correct but with some rather unique
> logic errors.

That has not been my experience.  I find I am more free to deal with logic
when the compiler is detecting the spelling errors.

> |> > It takes no longer
> |> > for an experienced C programmer to code a task in C than it would take an
> |> > experienced Ada programmer to do it in Ada.  And believe it or not, regardless
> |> > of the language used, programmers of the same level of experience will make
> |> > an equivalent number of mistakes.  It isn't the language that makes the
> |> > mistakes.

