The language requirements explicitly called for tasking at the language
level, hopefull to make programs more platform-neutral.  The implementor's
discretion in this case was necessary to get implementations on many
platforms.  In practice, you get one or more of the mechanisms the
underlying OS supports.  There are many multi-task problems where the
details of the tasking model don't matter.  Those can be implemented in
Ada without any platform-specific pragmas.

On the other hand, Ada does _not_ give the implementor any wiggle room on
how to evaluate a multi-part conditional expression.

A number of things in C are explicitly platform-dependent, i.e. the result
of a chunk of code is supposed to depend on the platform.  Ada aims the
other way, but often gives you a way to specify low-level details in a
platform-neutral way when you need to.  It tends to make the dependent
part more visible.


