Though I think sloppy coding is not sufficiently discouraged in the
schools these days, in any language.

1. It's best not to make the mistake in the first place.

2. Next best is to have the compiler catch the mistake.

3. Next best is to detect the mistake at run time.

4. Worst is to not detect the mistake at all, and generate wrong results.

My main claim is that Ada is much better at number 2 than C is.  The
compiler has much more information at it's fingertips, so this should not
be too surprising.  It seems to be well confirmed in practice.

Number 1 is probably largely cultural.  Good programmers are better at
number 1 than are bad programmers.


