In comp.sys.dec Malcolm Dunnett <nothome@spammers.are.scum> wrote:
>    I can't believe the cost of making the motherboard upgradeable would
> be anywhere near the cost of needing to replace the entire box to get
> a faster processor. As others have commented, many (most) PeeCee
> motherboards are upgradeable and that's in a very cost-sensitive market.

Well... it's not only that the CPU is soldered in. When I got our DS10
fall, it came with a dead RTC chip. (Good quality control, btw.) The
little black "Dallas" thingie is exactly the same clock-with-battery
as in many older PeeCee mainboards. For newer PeeCee maiboards they
already figured out that it makes sense to have a clock chip and an
exchangeable normal battery...

Anyway. I call Compaq, explain the problem: machine loses time on power
off. My diagnosis: RTC chip dead. Please exchange. _Now_. They send a
tech two days later. He brings a complete mainboard. He wants to
disassemble the DS10 immediately. I stop him and ask why, since it can
only be the RTC, and I want this over fast. He sees the technical reason,
but is unconvinced because of his part numbers. In the end he exchanged
only the RTC chip, and everything was fine. As he explained, in theory 
he _could_not_ change the battery as such, because it does not have a
Compaq spare part number. The whole mainboard is the spare part!!!

So, expect to carry USD 2,- to your friendly neighborhood electronics
store in a few years, or pay for a new DS10 mainboard if your battery
goes bad and you are not covered by a service contract.

Which leads to the question why it is not possible to have such cheap
"hardware repair only" arrangements for small Alphas like the CarePaq
for Proliant servers.

Wolfgang Rupp



