In article <3A9D58D3.92B3B87A@videotron.ca>, JF Mezei <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> writes:

> Is Java a compiled language, or a run-time environment ? Doesn't Java execute
> in its own virtual machine with lots of support from the Java engine ? Does
> this qualify as a true compiled language ?

Java is a programming language.  Sun has a compiler that produced normal
object code for their operating system.  VMS does not.

Two years ago I heard the Sun Chief Science Officer talking about the
benefits (sic) of Java, and mourning the fact that this sideline feature
of the byte code engine implementation was getting all the publicity and
hiding the glory of the language when used with a normal compiler.

I suppose Java looks good if you are coming from C,
and that was his premise.


