C/C++ is *bullshit*. Who does not accept this is either
uneducated or stupid. Both conditions exclude any reasonable
exchange of thoughts.

While the observation is correct that it is possible to use bad,
outdated, rotten languages like C/C++ to write good software (if
you are Superman) the time you need is the problem.

There are different levels of different dimensions of quality
aspects. 
For an OS the quality has to be high wrt bugs and speed. To
write an OS in assembly language you'll need a lot of time. I
assume that for VMS the implementation languages have been
selected carefully to reflect the balance between speed of
execution and speed of development. I assume this was the
compromise - not wrt the bugs.
For an office program the bugs are more annoying than the lack
of speed - "cum grano salis" as the Latin would say or better
would have said. To select C/C++ to implement anything useful is
very, very stupid because of the problems of the language which
aren't solved so far wrt compiler construction and
standardisation: there are no compilers available which come
close to implement the whole language definitions (both C and
C++) in an almost bug free and standardised way. Standardisation
of language features implementation is generally missing. This
is the reason why it's necessary to talk about language
specification and language feature implementation seperately.

There aren't enough people around to use these tools in a
reasonable way.

Too many projects failed because of using these languages. You
can drive a Rolls-Royce to death and it's your fault. But using
a pair of crutches while the others outran you driving fast
vehicles is frustrating. Don't do that.

While it's true that you can use good tools to write bad SW
StarOffice is an example of using bad tools *and* writing bad
SW. I think we all agree that StarOffice isn't an option. From
what I heard Corel's SW *is* one.


