I disagree.  You stated that using C/C++ was stupid (or something
to that affect) and that you couldn't write good code in it.  Therefore.
naming other languages and their appropriate use is necessary for
the discussion.  Since you won't suggest alternatives, you clearly
demonstrate an anti C/C++ bias that must be more emotionally
based than fact based.  I haven't even heard your objections to
C/C++ except their age.

Believe me, I am NOT a defender of C.  There are many things I HATE
about C.  Here's 4 of them:

        1. Case Sensitivity.  I am use to it, but I think it is a BIG
           problem.  tmp, TMP, Tmp, tMp, tmP, TMp, tMP should NOT refer
           to different things.

        2. SWITCH/CASE doesn't auto-break.  I NEVER depend on being able
           to fall through to the next CASE in a SWITCH statement.  The
           default should be BREAK and allow specifying CONTINUE if you
           want to drop through.  THIS IS A BIG PROBLEM!!!

        3. BREAK.  Break should be allowed from ALL Begin/End ({})
           constructs (including IF ELSE).  You should also be able to
           BREAK # where # is the number of Begin/End blocks you want
           to break out of.

        4. Use of "==" for compares.  It's WAY too easy to put in "="
           instead.  In fact, I usually do a specialized search of ALL
           my code before the first compile just to try and catch these.

However, C is currently one of the most practical development languages
because you have a large pool of people who know it and can be brought
in to a project to work on it.  This is VERY important in commercially
viable
software.  Also, it is portable if the programmer isn't too careless.  You
always
have to assume your code will need to run on another platform/OS.

My ideal language would probably be mostly a combination of BLISS and C.
However, I do mostly tools and System programming.  Therefore, I am not
probably
the best sample case for what makes a good programming language.  However, I
have
done just about every kind of programming from Accounting packages, to OS
code to
embedded systems to realtime.

One thing I don't want is a language to get in the way.  A lot of newer
languages are
designed to prevent programmers for doing things to keep them from making
mistakes.
I understand this logic, but what you have is an effort to over compensate
because
programmers were never taught to program well and they write BAD code.

In order for a language to be useable, they invariably provide some
"backdoor"
that allows you to do the things you really want to do.  As a result, those
"backdoors" get abused.  Along comes someone else who says they have to
do something new that removes that "backdoor".  As more code is written in
the
new language, it becomes clear that new "backdoors" have to be implemented
because the language is preventing complex things from being done or at
least
is making them so difficult that they take too long.  This was always my
problem
with ADA.  Time to implement a project is VERY important.  It's one of the
BAD
things with C/C++ in terms of applications development.

Progrmaming Languages are just part of the tool set programmers use to solve
problems.  What we really do is we solve problems.  The code writing is
merely
a clerical function through which we express those solutions.

Sure we should use better tools when they are there.  However, better
programming
languages will NEVER be a substitute for writing GOOD code.  The real
problem we
need to solve is making sure programmers learn how to write code well to
start with.
That is NOT being taught in Universities.  My own son is majoring in
computer engineering
and his professors DON'T have a clue how to write GOOD code.  I am teaching
him
that and he is juggling writing GOOD code with the CRAP the professors want.
Hopefully
he will learn from both and get the grades he wants and still be able to
write GOOD code
when he finally goes to work for someone.  However, his manager there won't
have a clue
either and will insist he write BAD code because he doesn't know how to
write GOOD code
and can't understand the benefits.

How many times do you still hear programmers justify the requirement that
all routines
exit at the bottom, or insist it is more efficient to write fewer large
routines than many
small routines to save a few routine calls.  As long as this ignorance
persists, all the
GREAT new programming languages won't solve the problem.

When I even thing about using a ELSE statement, I force myself to justify
it's use.
Most programmers think using ELSEs is really great and liberally do nested
IF then ELSEs.

Teach programmers to write code well, and new progrmaming languages will
evolve that help us solve the problems better and not ones that prevent us
from
doing things we shouldn't do in the first place unless they are REALLY
required.

just my opinions,


Rick Cadruvi...




