fair load).  For 3 months after installation the system ran like a dream.
Performance and reliability were excellent.

Then, a week ago, the systems appeared to hang for up to 10 minutes at a
time (in terms of the NFS clients).  On the systems themselves, all was
normal, except that ASE process on each box claimed that it had lost
ether comms to it's partner-box.  The switch reported no errors, no
interfaces up or down, etc.

This seems to happen at fairly modest loads, but only during office
hours.  And it happens a *lot* -- up to 40 times a day, making life
truly miserable.  The switch does not report heavy traffic, so I doubt
that it is the problem.

I noticed the new patch kit (t64v40gas0002-20010111.tar), and in
particular the description on one patch: "network failures for less than
7 seconds will no longer leave the ASE in an unknown state" or something
along those lines.  I grabbed the patch bundle and installed it.  While
the problem is definitely reduced, it still happens, now only about 5-10
times per day.

A colleague mentioned that replacing the network cards might affect the
situation, so I did in the one box, and it seemed to help.  I would have
done the same to the other box, but the only other spare card I have is
a DE600, which isn't recognised by DU4.0 :-<  (and not even with the new
patch kit... am I missing something?  I recall that it was a hard,
3-hour job to get one of these cards working on a DU 4.0F system,
updating the firmware, patching the kernel, etc, etc.  But never mind
all this...)

I've now officially run out of ideas.  Has any one seen anything like
this?  Our configuration does not deviate at all from the standard NFS
service under ASE/TruCluster (do they *have* to keep renaming everything?)

I'd be really grateful for any sort of feedback, even if it's "have you
checked the cabling?"

