This was the subject of a thread some time back. The DS10L CPU is
soldered in because of the extra height of a socket. But, as old 11
hands will remember, you can get frameless socket pins that go direct
into the board, and add no height.

If you dig out the original Alpha articles from Dec Tech Journal, on
of them was by Rayethon on their Mil Spec Alpha. They had to use a
socket and retainer. Soldering in a chip the size an an Alpha results
is very large thermal induced stress from the soldering process, and
can fracture the package. And 064s were smaller.

On the other hand, there is very little slack in modern systems.  If
you do go the upgrade route with the CPU chip, you quickly run into
problems with bus, memory and IO chipset speeds and the like. But, for
some people, with for instance extremly high cache hit rate and a very
small footprint, it is an atractive way to go.

Comparing a 600MHz DS20 with a Samsung 264E and about 32M of L3 on the
daughter card would be an interesting experiment.

