Hi Andrew,

I'm sure this has occurred to you, nevertheless I'll mention it.

You might try setting up a periodic ping (using "ping -c <count>" to
keep traffic down) and log the result.  Then you could check for
a correlation between real network problems and ASE's complaints.

If you aren't able to determine that the network really had problems,
then I would recommend escalating to digipaq.  It could be a bug.

One other thing to think about is the DE500s.  I know that when using certain
cards ("DE500-AA" und "-XA") in full-duplex mode ASE is not able to recognize
a lost link.  It could be that there is some other problem also related to
full-duplex mode which you are experiencing.  You might try turning off
full-duplex mode to see if that helps.  You will really have to force it
to be "off".  If you let the card auto-negotiate to full-duplex you'll be
back where you started.  You can, of course, configure dynamically with
"lan_config -i tu0 -x [0|1]".

You could also try the mickeysoft approach to solving the problem: re-install.
I mean, of course, that V5.1 is out and it's not intolerable buggy.  If you're
just serving NFS with the box, you could upgrade.  If you have apps that
depend
on V4, you're outta luck.  Between V4 and V5 the cluster stuff more-or-less
was completely replaced, so if you are in fact experiencing a bug, it's likely
that it won't be in V5.

oh yeah, and have you checked the cables? :-)

cheers,

Rob Urban

