If you remove all the memory, nothing will happen. 
The console (IIRC) needs *some* memory to say
anything at all.

It's been a while since I opened
up a VS 4000-9x but the memory does
not go in in any obvious order. The
correct order is *NOT* the first four
slots, the last four slots, or either
of the possible ways of doing
every other slot.

I don't have the manual handy
but it is something like:
IN, OUT, IN, OUT, OUT, IN, OUT, IN.

If you look very carefully by the
SIMM sockets you will see markings
like 0A, 1B, 0C etc.

Fill the four slots marked with a 
zero and you should have a 
valid memory config.

This all cropped up here sometime
in the last year or so, but since
deja (now google) is no longer
as usable as it was you may
have some difficulty finding the
information.

I can look it up in a few days if
you still cannot find it.

Antonio

