For me, that was the beginning of the end for DEC. Coming from a mainframe 
background, I initially found it hard to understand why DEC _sold_ software 
rather than renting it out. I quickly came to understand the attraction of that.

But then they started gouging customers. A particular example I recall is of a 
friend who wanted a 17 user system (1987?). DEC told him that he needed an 
unlimited user license because it was one more than 16. It put their price way 
above the competition. When he complained about that, they dropped the price by 
such a staggering amount that he felt insulted that they had tried to gouge him 
in the first place.

Result? One more unix customer, violently against DEC. Prominent in the local 
business community, I am sure he spread that message around.

Next result? I felt I couldn't recommend DEC/VMS to many other small to medium 
sized businesses. At that time I was seeing a lot of folks buying expensive 
DOS/Netware solutions when a simple MicroVAX with dumb VTs could have been much 
more cost effective.

> On the other hand, Oracle gets away nicely with its gouging price model based
> on "power units", so there's ample precedent.
> 
Back to publicity and user perception. Remember the huge salaries a few years 
ago commanded by folks with SAP knowledge? Everyone suddenly wanted that 
expertise and were prepared to commit their company's budgets to it. Huge 
loyalty bonuses followed that, often followed by the demise or takeover of said 
company.
