[...snip...]
> 
> Remember that the HSZ70 is itself a SCSI device, from the host's point of
> view.  Since every drive on one specific controller is getting the error, I
> suspect that the problem is with the controller itself, rather than the one of
> the drives behind the controller.
> 
>         Stu

My experience with the HSZ controllers has been that the served disks
whose error count in incremented is irrelavent (other than the fact
that it is served by the same HSZ controller). The SCSI protocol does
not provide the means (a la MSCP) to immediately deliver the error data
back to the VMS system when an IO fails. I believe that the error data
is bundled up with the return of the next succesful IO and thus gets
attributed to that disk. Error log packets will typically, however,
include an associated port and target which point to the offending
drive (someone please correct me if I'm incorrect with any of this).
Initially, in this particular case there was no pointer to the failing
disk. Anyway, by shuffling hardware, and preferred paths, I was able
to trace this one back to a failed slot in the Storage Works shelf.
Thanks.
-- 
- Jim


