In article <96lq7k$cee1@imsp212.netvigator.com>, <tfhkcyt2000@taifuji.com> writes:

>  I have installed a NCR scsi card
>in ALPHA server, it can be found
>in SRM screen, but can not be found
>on tru64 unix, which commnd I must
>use in tru64 to add a device driver
>for scsi card?

On V4 and earlier (you don't say what version you're running), a
new kernel has to be built to look for new I/O adapter.  If the
adapter is one which closely resembles a supported one, you may
not need a driver.  You may also want to run an extensive qualification
to verify the new adapter works to your satisfaction.  Many of the
adapters using NCR chips work with the same driver that supports
the KZPAA which we sell/sold.

If none of the existing drivers recognize the adapter, then you'll
want to go back to the vendor and see if they have a driver for
Tru64 UNIX.  The command(s) to install their driver will depend
on how they wrote the installation procedure.

How to boot the generic kernel to add new devices is described
in one system management guides.  All the guides are avaialble
online using the documentation CDROM.  The short version is:

1.  Shutdown.

2.  Boot /genvmunix:

	>>>boot -fl s -fi "genvmunix" ...

    Watch the messages to see if the new adapter is at least recognized.
    No need to continue if it isn't.

3.  Check and mount the local file systems:

	# /sbin/bcheckrc

4.  Build a new kernel:

	# doconfig

5.  Copy the new kernel to the root:

	# cp /vmunix /vmunix.sav
	# cp /sys/"HOSTNAME"/vmunix /

    I think doconfig will echo the path to the new kernel, but generally
    replace "HOSTNAME" with your hostname in all capital letters.

6.  Reboot.
