In article <95judl$2eq$1@sherrill.kiva.net>,
Cornerstone Information Systems <ciswired@sherrill.kiva.net> wrote:
>In article <3A7B3A96.2C9A86F8@ins-msi.com>,
>Jeff Campbell  <jcampbell@ins-msi.com> wrote:
>>No, uVAXII memory cards use the Qbus CD slots to assert the memory
>>address bits. There are no Qbus bus cycles. The ribbon cable connector
>>carries the data bytes in and out, 32 bits plus parity at a time,
>>between the CPU and the memory.
>
>How does this arrangement differ from the 11/83-style PMI?

IIRC, 11/83 PMI actually lives on the Qbus; it just uses the PMI as an
extra go-faster channel.  DMA goes over the Qbus just like it does with
normal Qbus memory.  If you put PMI memory cards below the CPU instead
of above, or use it with a non-PMI CPU, the PMI isn't connected but the
memory still works as Qbus memory, albeit slower than if the PMI was in
use.

With uVAX memory, there is no direct connection from the Qbus to memory;
DMA transfers from Qbus devices pass through the CPU card.  uVAX memory
cards, if they touch the Qbus at all (the double width, 1 Mbyte MS630-AA
plugs into a CD slot, allowing a normal Qbus card to be plugged into the
AB half of the slot), just pass grant signals through.  Thus, uVAX memory 
only works if it's attached to the appropriate CPU card, ie you can't use
an MS630 (uVAX II memory) with a KA650 (uVAX III) CPU either.

-- don


