Hi,

Some time ago I started a thread about cdroms that work with
DEC hardware. Allthough the results were not so impressiv and
normally I would not publish it, I got mails from people asking
me what cdrom they should get for their old DEC workstation.
So I guess it will be usefull anyway.
If not, it will be just yet another piece of junk online ;-)
But I really hope it's usefull.


---------cut here------------------------------------------------------


Inofficial and incomplete list of third party cdroms that work with DEC's.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This list is maintained by Dennis Grevenstein. email contact info
is "cdromlist@pcde.inka.de"
You can find an up to date version of this document here:
http://sites.inka.de/pcde/dec-cdrom-list.txt

I. Introduction

The question "what cdrom will work with my old DEC?" is heard often.
The problem is that non-x86 workstations and especially DEC's will
normally need special cdroms that support special features.
Many vendors released their own branded cdroms for their hardware.
But you will have to pay much more money to get a slow DEC
branded cdrom for your workstation than for a fast modern or a
real cheap old standard cdrom drive.
So here is some sort of an answer for all who are looking for a
third party cdrom for a DEC workstation.
If the list gets longer(I hope so), I may release a html version.


II. Some general information.

As most Unix workstations, DEC's will need a cdrom that is capable
of reading 512 bytes/sector, but most PC style cdrom's can only read
2048 bytes/sector. Often there is jumper to enable this mode.
If not, the cdrom must support the "MODE SELECT" command that is
send by the PROM to enable the 512 bytes/sector mode.
But often, even if a cdrom supports all of the above and can be used
with other workstation-platforms e.g. Sun and SGI, the cdrom will not
work with DEC hardware.


III. drives that are known to work.

I decided to differentiate between VAX, MIPS based DECstations and Alpha
based workstations because of some irregularities. The Alpha section is
pretty small right now and Alphas should be compatible with cdroms in
the VAX or DECstation section, but a general rule could be:
If a drive is not listed as working in your category, don't get it.


III.1   VAX

Vendor   model #      operating system    hardware tested
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Apple    CD300e       VMS                 VAXstation, Infoserver
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
HP       C2944D       OpenVMS 6.2         uVAX 3100, VAXstation 4000/90,
                      OpenVMS 7.1         4000/60, 4000/VLC
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pioneer  DR-U124X     OpenVMS 7.2         uVAX 3100-80
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pioneer  DR-U12X      OpenVMS 7.2         uVAX 3100-30
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pioneer  DR-466       OpenVMS 7.1         VAXstation 3100-40
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Plextor  12/20        OpenVMS 7.2         uVAX 3100-80
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Plextor  PX-32TSi     OpenVMS 7.1         uVAX3100, VAXstation 4000/60
                                          VAXstation 4000/90
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sony     CDU561       Ultrix 4.5          uVAX 3100
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Toshiba  XM5701TA     OpenVMS 7.1         VAXstation 3100
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Toshiba  XM-3401B     OpenVMS 7.1         VAXstation 3100-40
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------


III.2   DECstations

DECstations seem to be really tricky, especially if you want to install
Ultrix. As far as i know Ultrix/MIPS can only be installed using an
original DEC cdrom. Typically your DECstation will boot of a compatible
cdrom, but the install programm will crash. If it is not compatible with
a DECstation the boot will fail completely.
I can't say much about NetBSD, but my DR-466 does not work with NetBSD
1.4.1 at all, but works fine with NetBSD 1.5. Other people said that
some older Toshiba cdroms work with VAXen, but not with DECstations.

Vendor   model #      operating system    hardware tested
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Plextor  PX-12CSi     NetBSD 1.5          DECstation 5000/133
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Plextor  PX-32TSi     NetBSD              DECstation 5000/33
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pioneer  DR-466       NetBSD 1.5          DECstation 5000/150
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------



III.3   Alpha

Vendor   model #      operating system    hardware tested
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Plextor  PX-40TSi     VMS                 Multia
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Apple    CD300e       VMS                 DEC3000
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------


IV. Other resources

The Sun cdrom FAQ may be useful. It provides some general information,
allthough this FAQ does not care, if a cdrom works with DEC hardware.

http://saturn.tlug.org/suncdfaq


V. thanks, suggestions and all the other things...

Many thanks to all who contributed to build up this list.
This list is by no means complete. So if you have ever used a non-DEC
cdrom that is not shown here with a DEC workstation, please send some
information to "cdromlist@pcde.inka.de". If you have other suggestions
or complains, feel free to mail them too.


